## Grammar / Tense / Agreement Issues

1. **"might seem to an unwarranted assault"** — Incorrect preposition. Should be "might seem **an** unwarranted assault" (or "might seem **to be** an unwarranted assault").
2. **"I had no ounce of doubt that they can cut costs"** — Tense mismatch. "had no doubt" is past; "can cut" is present. Should be "**could** cut" (or "would be able to cut").
3. **"as we've seen with their (GOOG mainly) first round of layoffs in early 2023"** — "first round" is wrong; the 2023 Google layoffs were the **second** round (the first was 2022). Also, "GOOG mainly" is awkward parenthetical placement.
4. **"McKinsey Pichai"** — This is a garbled name. You likely mean **Satya Nadella** (Microsoft CEO) or **Andy Jassy** (Amazon CEO). "McKinsey" is a consulting firm; "Pichai" is Sundar Pichai (Google CEO). The sentence is confusing as written.
5. **"Those who read history knows"** — Subject-verb agreement error. "Those who read history" is plural → should be "**know**."
6. **"every industry goes through this phrase"** — "phrase" is wrong; should be "**phase**" or "**cycle**."
7. **"Abundance in isolated, privileged pockets isn't True Abundance"** — Capitalizing "True Abundance" mid-sentence is ungrammatical unless it's a proper noun/title. Should be lowercase.
8. **"the labor supply/demand reaches equilibrium"** — "supply/demand" is treated as a compound subject; "reaches" should be "**reach**" (or "the labor supply/demand balance reaches").
9. **"salary to be monotonic-increasing"** — "monotonic-increasing" is awkward and non-idiomatic. Should be "monotonically increasing" or "always increasing."
10. **"I always knew we're in a state of flux"** — Tense mismatch. "knew" is past; "we're" is present. Should be "we **were** in a state of flux" (or "we are").
11. **"the party's gonna end sooner or later"** — "gonna" is informal; "the party's" is a contraction of "the party is" but reads oddly. Consider "the party is about to end."
12. **"I can't accurately predict what GOOG will be like in say 15 years"** — "in say 15 years" is awkward; "in about 15 years" is cleaner.
13. **"the ruthless capitalist engine is taking over to ensure financial 'efficiency'"** — "engine" is a metaphor; "is taking over" is personification. Consider whether this is intentional stylistic choice or a grammar error.
14. **"with promises of revolutionary tech largely gone (besides small pockets of AI, which in practice currently is operationally much smaller than general tech)"** — "in practice currently is operationally much smaller" is redundant and unclear. "operationally" is vague here.
15. **"I live so frugally"** — "live" should be "live" (correct), but "so frugally" is vague. Consider "I live so frugally" → "I live so frugally" is fine; the issue is "so frugally" is vague.

## Serious Stylistic / Clarity Issues

1. **"Andreessen Horowitz basically saying people in big tech companies are not doing enough real work to justify their existence"** — "basically saying" is vague; "not doing enough real work to justify their existence" is confusing. Consider "Andreessen Horowitz arguing that people at large tech companies aren't producing enough genuine work to justify their salaries."
2. **"I can't help but agree that he's probably onto something, at least from the capitalist perspective"** — "at least from the capitalist perspective" is vague. Consider "at least from a purely capitalist perspective."
3. **"I briefly wondered whether it would be a good idea to buy some at the relatively low prices -- in particular META and GOOG"** — "some" is vague; "at the relatively low prices" is redundant. Consider "I briefly considered buying META and GOOG at their relatively low prices."
4. **"My analysis back then was exactly on the same line of logic -- while META and GOOG (and particularly GOOG) don't seem to be capable of creating new products and finding new avenues of growth"** — "exactly on the same line of logic" is redundant; "don't seem to be capable of creating new products and finding new avenues of growth" is vague. Consider "My analysis was based on the same logic: META and GOOG appear unable to create new products or find new avenues of growth."
5. **"it seems their monopolies are still functional and they can probably squeeze quite a bit of profit from the monopolies before things break down"** — "monopolies are still functional" is vague; "squeeze quite a bit of profit from the monopolies" is awkward. Consider "their monopolies remain profitable and can likely generate substantial profits before they collapse."
6. **"they can always layoff more people to reduce costs and increase margins and profit"** — "layoff" should be "lay off" (two words); "reduce costs and increase margins and profit" is redundant. Consider "they can always lay off more people to cut costs and boost margins."
7. **"I had no ounce of doubt that they can cut costs by almost half without really affecting day to day operations"** — "no ounce of doubt" is an idiom; "day to day" should be "day-to-day." Consider "I had no doubt that they could cut costs by nearly half without disrupting day-to-day operations."
8. **"McKinsey Pichai is definitely ready to do whatever pleases the shareholders in this regard"** — "in this regard" is vague; "whatever pleases the shareholders" is awkward. Consider "Satya Nadella is clearly willing to do whatever maximizes shareholder value."
9. **"The only risk is basically regulatory (antitrust), but In America We Trust they're capitalist enough to not kill their golden goose"** — "In America We Trust" is a phrase, not a name; "they're capitalist enough" is awkward. Consider "The only risk is regulatory (antitrust), but given 'In America We Trust,' they're unlikely to kill their golden goose."
10. **"There's also a concern whether the companies will remain healthy enough for investors (as a whole) to recoup their capital investments before the companies crash and burn"** — "whether" should be "that" (or "whether" is acceptable but "that" is cleaner); "crash and burn" is an idiom; "investors (as a whole)" is awkward. Consider "There's also a concern that the companies won't remain healthy enough for investors to recoup their investments before they collapse."
11. **"the free money is draining away"** — "free money" is a metaphor; "draining away" is vague. Consider "the easy money is running out."
12. **"the ruthless capitalist engine is taking over to ensure financial 'efficiency'"** — "ruthless capitalist engine" is a metaphor; "financial 'efficiency'" is vague. Consider "capitalist forces are driving the market toward ruthless efficiency."
13. **"This necessarily involves layoffs and wage cuts/stagnation until the labor supply/demand reaches equilibrium (i.e. these jobs are going to be as great/shitty as any random gig in the general job market)"** — "great/shitty" is slang; "random gig" is vague. Consider "This necessarily involves layoffs and wage stagnation until the labor market reaches equilibrium (i.e., these jobs will be as good/bad as any random job in the general market)."
14. **"product enshittification until antitrust slams down hard"** — "enshittification" is a neologism; "antitrust slams down hard" is vague. Consider "product degradation until antitrust regulators intervene forcefully."
15. **"People in more mature industries generally have a reasonable expectation what their careers would look like in say 20 years"** — "expectation what" is a grammar error; should be "expectation **of what**." "in say 20 years" is awkward; "in about 20 years" is cleaner.
16. **"especially as I'm geographically misplaced (there's actually no tech jobs here)"** — "geographically misplaced" is vague; "there's actually no tech jobs here" has a subject-verb agreement error ("there's" should be "there are"). Consider "especially given that I'm in a location with no tech jobs."
17. **"my technical expertise is... in some funny stack that nobody in their right mind uses"** — "funny stack" is vague; "nobody in their right mind uses" is awkward. Consider "my technical expertise is in a niche stack that few people use."
18. **"we're still sane though, just unique circumstances"** — "we're still sane though" is vague; "just unique circumstances" is awkward. Consider "we're still functional, just in unusual circumstances."

